\use{document}

\title {Splitting a Column with vsplit}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {One column, cut in two}

The \texttt{vsplit} primitive cuts a saved vertical box at a page-style
breakpoint --- the latest legal break no taller than a target height --- handing
back the top piece and leaving the remainder in the register for the next cut.
It is the building block under split footnotes and column balancing. Below, a
single tall paragraph is typeset into a narrow \texttt{vbox}, split near its
middle, and the two halves are set side by side as a pair of columns.

\set hsize {170}
\setbox col \vbox{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Typesetting a paragraph this narrow
forces many short lines, which gives the column builder plenty of legal
breakpoints --- one between every pair of lines --- so a split can land almost
anywhere. When the cut is made, everything above the chosen break becomes the top
piece and everything below it stays behind, ready to flow into the next column or
the next page. That is exactly how a page builder fills one page and carries the
rest onto the following one, and it is how a balanced two-column layout divides a
galley into equal halves.}
\set hsize {460}

\vskip 6pt

\set colheight {\ht col}
\setbox colA \vsplit col to:{\calc{\colheight / 2}}

\hbox{\box colA \hskip 24 \box col}

\vfill\eject
